  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 AM CDT Fri Jul 24 2026\n\nStratus lingers east river this morning, maybe a few pockets of fog \ntoo, and still somewhat comfortable temperatures and humidity. \nFocus for the next several days include a severe weather threat for \nlater this evening and multi-day heatwave. \n\nThe 500mb pattern continues to be defined by broad upper ridging \nacross the center of the country. Currently were under northwest \nflow around the high, which allows for this very subtle wave this \nevening. The ridge axis builds overhead with increasing heights for \nthe Northern Plains though we retain a northwest component with the \nridge axis along the Dakota/MT/WY border. The upper high gradually \nretrogrades into a more traditions 4-Corners high with a deepening \ntrough across the eastern CONUS. \n\n700mb temperatures on the increase today out ahead of the shortwave, \nand we'll get up to around +9 to +12C today.  Those temperatures \ncontinue to ramp-up for the weekend, +13C to +18C in the GFS (which \nmixes well past this level both days) but +12C to +16C in the other \ndeterministic models. This is roughly 2 standard deviations above \nclimo, 500mb temperatures are also a standard deviation above climo. \nWhile we'll see \"relief\" from this extreme heat, 700mb temperatures \nare still a standard deviation above climo for all of next week. \nThus temperatures will average this weekend 10F to 20F degrees above \nclimo, and 5F to 15F for next week. The one thing that we cant \nquantify, and remains as a generalization at this point is what\nimpact high smoke will have on temperatures. \n\nCAMS still attempting to generate storms along a boundary up across \nnorthwest/north central South Dakota late this afternoon/evening. \nConvective modus operandi is something like high based storms, up \naround 10kft, with skinny CAPE and dry atmosphere up above 500mb. \nThus, it'll be outflow winds and with such a strong CAP, they'll \nprobably blow themselves out despite moving relatively quickly with \n40-60kts in the convective layer.  As such, not expecting much \noverall moisture potential. HREF member postage stamps 24 hour \nrainfall not showing much for the CWA save an isolated streak or two \nof 1/4\" to 1/2\". \n\nSide note. Looks for some Coteau winds with a southwest oriented low \nlevel jet. 1/2 km winds are about 40kts tonight. \n\nVery deep mixing despite warm temperatures aloft, can't rule out an \nisolated daytime driven storm Saturday evening, but more likely is \nthe potential for elevated convection associated with a plume of \nmoisture aloft coming around the high from the Intermountain West. \nThat same wave is responsible for the Day 3 risk area in \nMinnesota which features more of a pulse convection regime under\nweaker although unidirectional steering flow. \n\nAs for heat headlines, still a little too soon for an advisory, but \ndeterministic NBM is well into criteria both Saturday and Sunday. \n25th-75th percentile ranges in highs is 3-8F for both days though \nthis is removing the higher biased (overly mixy) GEFS. 25th-75th \nrange in NBM dewpoints also indicates quite a bit of uncertainty at \n13 to 20F (mid 50s to low 70F). Aside from showing a likewise overly \nmixy GEFS bias, additional uncertainty added as corn is at its \npotential peak ET, but being tempered by the regional lack of \nconsistent moisture. Looking at just an EC/Canadian ensemble \nsuggests dewpoints may end up being a little underdone in the NBM.",
